For what value of $x$ does $g(x) = 0$?', NULL, '[{"key":"A","text":"$\\\\frac{5}{2}$"},{"key":"B","text":"$3$"},{"key":"C","text":"$\\\\frac{7}{2}$"},{"key":"D","text":"$\\\\frac{11}{2}$"}]'::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The correct answer is C. Using the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, -1)$, the slope is $\\frac{-1 - 5}{4 - 1} = \\frac{-6}{3} = -2$. The equation is $g(x) = -2(x - 1) + 5 = -2x + 7$. Setting $g(x) = 0$: $-2x + 7 = 0$, so $x = \\frac{7}{2}$. Verification: $g(-2) = -2(-2) + 7 = 11$, $g(1) = -2(1) + 7 = 5$, and $g(4) = -2(4) + 7 = -1$, all matching the table. Option A ($\\frac{5}{2}$) results from solving $-2x + 5 = 0$, using only the $y$-value at $x = 1$ without finding the full equation. Option B ($3$) is an arithmetic slip, rounding $\\frac{7}{2}$ down. Option D ($\\frac{11}{2}$) comes from using $11$ as the constant term in the equation.', '{"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"}}'::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, '{"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-07-20"}'::jsonb, '{"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-07-20","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}'::jsonb);



























